> etc). The answer was, more or less, 'yes.'
>
> So having read spoilers and whatnot, and STILL being pretty clueless on
> how to use pets properly, I turn to you, the RGRN elite. Are there any
> pearls of wisdom on using pets that I should really know to use them
> effectively, so I can play better as characters that struggle in the
> beginning (e.g., Wizards)?
>
> Thanks!

- Carrying around tripe rations makes your (cat/dog) pets want to stay
near you more. (ever notice how they dart for the tripe on the floor as
soon as they see it?) This helps a lot with the stairs issue, etc. If
you find a general store or delicatessen, buy all the tripe it has as
soon as possible.

- Magic whistles make the above point basically useless, but they can
be hard to find. They work on non-cat/dog pets though, which makes them
more important later.

- Two of the most important uses for pets in the early game is BUC
identifying and stealing from shops. For the first, throwing a bunch of
items down in a corner and waiting for your pet to walk over them is
basically the idea... the fewer places for the pet to step, the higher
the chance for the "reluctantly" message. For the second, it just takes
patience, although one of the above two methods can speed this up
greatly. There are spoilers available for tips on stealing from shops,
and there was a large discussion on credit cloning here a few days
ago... both would be good reading.

- Keep in mind the limitations of your pets: your starting pet is only
worth using for combat until you get to around dlvl10 or so, even full
grown. If you're interested in keeping pets beyond that, look for poly
traps, chameleons, /oPoly, etc., or charm monster/taming. A good rule
of thumb is that if it isn't at least a moderate danger to your life,
it probably isn't worth keeping as a combat oriented pet (there are
exceptions to this, such as purple worms).

- Pets heal much slower than you do, so stethoscopes and healing
potions/spells are very important the deeper you go. This is made
easier by the fact that all of full healing, extra healing, healing,
restore ability, and gain ability act as full healing when you hit your
pet with them (thrown or wielded). Also, late game pets which are
spellcasters tend to live longer, since they can heal themselves.

- Keeping track of things that affect your pet's tameness are also
important. They get more tame every time they eat (which is why pets
that don't eat are sometimes difficult to keep), and get less tame
whenever you hit them, displace them into traps, ride them (unless you
are a knight) and for every 150 turns you spend on a different dungeon
level (there might be other things I'm missing here). Make sure you
keep their tameness up.

Hope this helps,
